  "kind": "crypto.signature-verify",
  "description": "Golden RSA PKCS#1 v1.5 signature-verification vectors over \u00a76.4 signing preimages, under the Persona Version v0 signature algorithm SHA256withRSA (\u00a78; the algorithm name comes from the v0 policy, never hard-coded prose). Every 'preimageHex' is pinned byte-exact by an envelope.signing-preimage vector cited in the vector's description, so the chain payload fields -> preimage -> signature is provable end-to-end across languages. RSA PKCS#1 v1.5 is deterministic, so the goldens are stable and portable. This kind is VERIFY-ONLY by deliberate policy: the suite ships no private-key material (repository security policy, see the identity.pem kind), so the sign side cannot be goldenized portably \u2014 the fixture RSA-2048 private key (and the second key pair used for the different-key vector) were generated once, used to derive these goldens, and destroyed; only the public key (PEM, X.509 SubjectPublicKeyInfo), the preimage inputs and the golden signature bytes are committed. Accept vectors assert the boolean verdict of verification (expected true/false); the single reject vector pins that an unknown algorithm name must be rejected with an error per \u00a78 + \u00a711 rule 3.",
